FUNCTION MAPSPEC(FN, WANT)
* mapfield — build a %MAP% spec from a FILE'S DICTIONARY, rather than field by
* field.  GPL-2.0-only.
*
*   SPEC = MAPSPEC("CUSTOMERS", "")        every mappable D-item
*   SPEC = MAPSPEC("CUSTOMERS", FIELDS)    only these (@AM-delimited names)
*
* Returns the same spec format MAPFIELD builds and json consumes:
* name<VM>attr<VM>conv<VM>type<VM>assoc, one field per attribute.  Empty when
* the dictionary cannot be opened.
*
* WHY: a spec assembled by hand is a SECOND VOCABULARY -- whatever names that
* programmer chose, in that program.  Taken from the dictionary, the names are
* the file's own, so a LIST, the SQL projection, json and a template all say
* CUST.NAME and mean the same thing (#24).
*
* IT CALLS MAPFIELD PER FIELD rather than formatting the spec itself, so the
* conv -> type derivation has exactly one implementation.  Change the rules
* there and this follows.
*
* --- what counts as mappable ---------------------------------------------
* ONLY D-ITEMS THAT NAME AN ATTRIBUTE.  An I-descriptor is a computed field,
* not a stored one; resolving it would mean evaluating the descriptor, which is
* both a bigger job and a way for a consumer to run arbitrary dictionary code.
* PH phrases are not fields at all.  Both are skipped, and that is a stated
* limitation rather than an oversight.
*
* --- the attribute the association lives in ------------------------------
* THE LAYOUTS DIFFER, AND NOT WHERE YOU WOULD GUESS.  mvx's D-item is
*
*     1 D   2 attr#   3 conv   4 heading   5 format   6 assoc
*
* while UniData's and UniVerse's is
*
*     1 D   2 loc     3 conv   4 name      5 format   6 S/M   7 assoc
*
* (mvx docs/open-dict.md).  They agree on 1-5 and diverge at 6: mvx puts the
* association there, the others put single/multi there and the association at
* 7.  jBASE's shape is documented nowhere I could find.
*
* So this does not branch on platform.  It takes the association from whichever
* of 7 and 6 carries a NAME -- an association name is never "S" or "M", which
* is exactly what the other layout puts there.  One rule, no $IFDEF, and it is
* right on both documented shapes without being told which it is reading.
   DEFFUN MAPFIELD(A, B, C, D, E)
   SPEC = "" ; KEYS = ""
   IF FN = "" THEN RETURN("")
   OPEN "DICT", FN TO DFV ELSE RETURN("")
*
* Either the names asked for, or every id in the dictionary.
   IDS = ""
   IF WANT # "" THEN
      IDS = WANT
   END ELSE
*     A SELECT-LIST VARIABLE, pre-assigned, with LOOP/ELSE EXIT.  Every part of
*     that spelling is load-bearing: UniVerse rejects a numbered list ("option
*     'S' requires variable name") and will not parse LOOP WHILE READNEXT, and
*     UniData prints "UNINITIALIZED VARIABLE USED!" into the caller's own output
*     if the list variable is not assigned first (mv_git settled this spelling).
      DLIST = ""
      SELECT DFV TO DLIST
      LOOP
         READNEXT DID FROM DLIST ELSE EXIT
         IDS<-1> = DID
      REPEAT
   END
*
   NI = DCOUNT(IDS, @AM)
   FOR I = 1 TO NI
      DID = IDS<I>
      IF DID = "" THEN CONTINUE
      READ DREC FROM DFV, DID THEN
         DTYPE = OCONV(TRIM(DREC<1>), "MCU")
         IF DTYPE = "D" THEN
            LOC = TRIM(DREC<2>)
*           A D-item whose location is not a number does not name an attribute
*           -- some dictionaries carry D-items pointing at the record id with a
*           non-numeric marker.  Skipped rather than guessed at.
            IF LOC # "" AND NUM(LOC) THEN
               CONV = TRIM(DREC<3>)
*              The association, from whichever attribute holds a name.
               ASSOC = TRIM(DREC<7>)
               IF ASSOC = "" THEN
                  A6 = OCONV(TRIM(DREC<6>), "MCU")
                  IF A6 # "" AND A6 # "S" AND A6 # "M" THEN ASSOC = TRIM(DREC<6>)
               END
*              SORTED BY ATTRIBUTE, so the spec mirrors the record's own layout
*              and two runs over one dictionary agree -- SELECT makes no promise
*              about order, and a spec whose field order moves between runs
*              makes every encoded document differ for no reason.
*              LOCATE Format 1 (parenthesised) with "AL": the flavour-dependent
*              IN...SETTING form is not portable.
               LOCATE(LOC, KEYS; POS; "AL") ELSE NULL
               KEYS = INSERT(KEYS, POS, 0, 0, LOC)
               SPEC = INSERT(SPEC, POS, 0, 0, MAPFIELD(DID, LOC, CONV, "", ASSOC))
            END
         END
      END
   NEXT I
   RETURN(SPEC)
